_model: proposition
---
title: Proposition 14
---
order: 14
---
short_label: XI.14
---
statement: Planes to which the same straight line is at right angles are parallel.
---
proof:

Let any straight line *AB* be at right angles to each of the planes *CD* and *EF.*

I say that the planes are parallel.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="310" height="200" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="XI.14 diagram" src="propXI14.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "XI.14",
        elements: [
            "X;point;free;100000,200",
            "Y;point;free;200,-100000",
            "Z;point;free;40000,-100000",
            "C;point;free;160,40;black;0,75,100",
            "D;point;free;40,100;black;0,75,100",
            "C1;point;intersection;C,X,D,Z;0;0",
            "D1;point;intersection;D,X,C,Z;0;0",
            "P;polygon;quadrilateral;C,C1,D,D1;0;0;gray;brighter",
            "F;point;free;160,120;black;0,75,100",
            "E;point;free;40,180;black;0,75,100",
            "F1;point;intersection;F,X,E,Z;0;0",
            "E1;point;intersection;E,X,F,Z;0;0",
            "Q;polygon;quadrilateral;F,F1,E,E1;0;0;gray;brighter",
            "A;point;free;100,75",
            "B;point;lineSlider;A,Y,100,145",
            "AB;line;connect;A,B",
            "G;point;free;270,30",
            "H;point;lineSlider;G,Z,210,170",
            "GH;line;connect;G,H",
            "K;point;lineSlider;GH,240,125",
            "AK;line;connect;A,K",
            "BK;line;connect;B,K"
        ],
    });
</script>
</figure>

For, if not, then they meet when produced. Let them meet. Then they intersect as a straight line. Let it be *GH.* [!just XI.Def.8, XI.3]

Take a point *K* at random on *GH,* and join *AK* and *BK.*

Now, since *AB* is at right angles to the plane *EF,* therefore *AB* is also at right angle to *BK* which is a straight line in the plane *EF* produced. Therefore the angle *ABK* is right. For the same reason the angle *BAK* is also right. [!just XI.Def.3]

Thus, in the triangle *ABK* the sum of the two angles *ABK* and *BAK* equals two right angles, which is impossible. [!just I.17]

Therefore the planes *CD* and *EF* do not meet when produced. Therefore the planes *CD* and *EF* are parallel. [!just XI.Def.8]

Therefore, *planes at right angles to the same straight line are parallel.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
---
body:

Part of this proof is unnecessary. The line *GH* is irrelevant. If the two points meet, then they meet at some point, so the point *K* might just as well be taken as a point where they meet. The proof should, however, include another case, and that is where the given line meets both given planes at a point common to both planes.

#### Use of this proposition

This proposition is used in the proof of the next one.
